{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"unfair-jocund-giraffe","creation_channel":"cli","description":"## Symptom\nEvery `rebar transition ... closed` now detaches a compaction sweep (`rebar._commands.compact_trigger.run_sweep`, landed in `e0a55d5fac` / `d2dea317ec`). Observed today: a sweep held `.tickets-tracker/.ticket-write.lock` for **24+ minutes**, during which every rebar write host-wide failed:\n\n```\nflock: could not acquire lock after 181s (holder: pid=57999 held=47s pid_state=live)\nWARNING rebar._store.lock: write lock still held after 60s (pass 1/3) — retrying\n```\n\nIt blocked a `set-file-impact`, then a `transition ... closed` (which aborted with `could not acquire lock` after its completion verification had already run). A second sweep — spawned by a DIFFERENT worktree's close (`ci-config-schema-gate`) — did the same a few minutes later, so this is host-wide and recurring, not one bad run.\n\n## The sweep is not hung; it is quadratic-ish\nThe process sits at **0.0% CPU** because it is I/O-bound on git. It spawns one child at a time:\n\n```\ngit -c log.showSignature=false -C <tracker> log --diff-filter=A --full-history --no-renames \\\n    --format=%H -- '*<event-id>*.json'\n```\n\nThat is a `--full-history` pathspec walk of the ENTIRE tracker history, with a LEADING-WILDCARD glob (so no pathspec fast path), run once PER EVENT. Cost scales as (events to fold) x (history length), and both grow with the store.\n\n## Why this contradicts the feature's own design\n`run_sweep`'s docstring is explicit that compaction \"is optional housekeeping and must yield to work someone is waiting on, never compete with it\", and that \"a skipped sweep is a no-op for correctness (the events stay live and the next trigger folds them)\".\n\nThe stand-aside probe (`_lock.write_lock_is_busy`) implements that — but it runs **ONCE, before** taking the store lock. After that the sweep holds the lock for its whole multi-minute run, so a writer arriving one second later waits the full duration. The guard prevents the sweep from STARTING during contention; nothing makes it yield to contention that arrives afterwards.\n\nThe design's own correctness argument is what makes the fix cheap: because an abandoned sweep is a no-op, the sweep is free to bail at any point.\n\n## Suggested directions (not prescriptive)\n- **Re-probe and yield mid-sweep**: check `write_lock_is_busy` between units and abort when a writer is waiting. Aligns with the stated contract and is correctness-free.\n- **Do not hold the store lock across the whole sweep**: take it per fold, or fold into a scratch area and take it only to commit.\n- **Fix the per-event git walk**: one `git log --diff-filter=A --format='%H' --name-only` pass building an id->commit index costs one history walk total instead of one per event.\n- **Bound the work per trigger** so a large backlog drains over several sweeps rather than one long hold.\n\n## Impact\nCloses, `set-file-impact`, comments and reviews all block behind it, and the failure mode reads as a mysterious multi-minute hang rather than 'housekeeping is running'. In this session it required manually SIGTERMing sweeps twice to make progress — safe per the design's own no-op guarantee, but not something every operator will know.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] A foreground store write arriving while a sweep is running acquires the lock within seconds rather than waiting for the sweep to finish; verified by a test that starts a sweep over a store with pending folds and asserts a concurrent write completes promptly.\n- [ ] The sweep detects a waiting writer mid-run and stands down, leaving the store consistent and the unfolded events live (the documented no-op outcome).\n- [ ] Event->commit resolution does not run one `git log --full-history` per event; verified by asserting the number of git invocations does not scale with the event count.\n- [ ] A sweep interrupted at any point leaves no partial state that a later sweep cannot fold.","id":"b3a2-2268-c391-43df","parent_id":"","priority":2,"tags":["compaction","store","concurrency"],"ticket_type":"bug","title":"Out-of-band compaction sweep holds the shared store write lock for many minutes"},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1786652878840657001,"uuid":"764b6c42-3973-49f7-b036-77e81d108cbc"}